# Architecture Conventions

## NestJS: module placement
All feature modules live under `src/modules/<feature>/`. Never create module files directly under `src/`.

```
// ❌ BAD
src/characters/characters.module.ts

// ✅ GOOD
src/modules/characters/characters.module.ts
```

## NestJS: one class per file
Never put a `@Module` and a `@Injectable()` service in the same file. Each class gets its own file.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — service and module in configuration.module.ts
@Injectable()
export class ConfigurationService { ... }

@Module({ providers: [ConfigurationService] })
export class ConfigurationModule {}

// ✅ GOOD
// configuration.service.ts
@Injectable()
export class ConfigurationService { ... }

// configuration.module.ts
@Module({ providers: [ConfigurationService], exports: [ConfigurationService] })
export class ConfigurationModule {}
```

# SOLID Principles

## S — Single Responsibility
Each class/module does one thing. Split when a class has multiple reasons to change.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
class UserService {
  save(user: User) { /* db logic */ }
  sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) { /* email logic */ }
}

// ✅ GOOD
class UserRepository { save(user: User) {} }
class UserMailer { sendWelcomeEmail(user: User) {} }
```

## O — Open/Closed
Open for extension, closed for modification. Prefer adding new classes over editing existing ones.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — add a new type → edit this function
function getArea(shape: { type: string }) {
  if (shape.type === 'circle') { ... }
  if (shape.type === 'rect') { ... }
}

// ✅ GOOD
interface Shape { area(): number }
class Circle implements Shape { area() { ... } }
class Rect implements Shape { area() { ... } }
```

## L — Liskov Substitution
Subtypes must be usable wherever their parent type is expected without breaking behaviour.

## I — Interface Segregation
Prefer small, focused interfaces over large general ones. Clients should not depend on methods they don't use.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
interface Worker { work(): void; eat(): void; }

// ✅ GOOD
interface Workable { work(): void; }
interface Eatable  { eat(): void;  }
```

## D — Dependency Inversion
Depend on abstractions, not concretions. Inject dependencies rather than instantiating them.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
class OrderService {
  private mailer = new SmtpMailer();
}

// ✅ GOOD
class OrderService {
  constructor(private readonly mailer: Mailer) {}
}
```

# Class Member Ordering

Place all `private` methods after all `public` methods.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
class UserService {
  private validate(user: User) { ... }
  create(user: User) { ... }
  findAll() { ... }
}

// ✅ GOOD
class UserService {
  create(user: User) { ... }
  findAll() { ... }

  private validate(user: User) { ... }
}
```
